---
title: Invariants
description: A compiler and auto-regressive runtime for better LLM structured outputs.
date: 2026-08-18
href: https://github.com/ujaandas/invariants
---
Most structured output tooling for LLMs only constrains the *shape* of a response - valid JSON, a string here, a number
there - and leaves everything else to the prompt. Ask for a `total_price` next to a `unit_price` and a `quantity`, and
nothing stops the model from generating numbers that don't actually multiply out. You just hope, validate after the
fact, and maybe regenerate and hope again.

**invariants** treats that as a compiler problem instead of a prompting problem. You write a schema in a small DSL -
fields, types, and invariants like `this.total == this.unit_price * this.quantity` - and it gets compiled ahead of time
into a dependency graph over the fields, then reduced to an execution schedule. At generation time, the runtime walks
that schedule field by field: if a field is fully determined by fields already generated, it just computes it and skips
the model for that field entirely; otherwise, it masks the model's logits token by token so only tokens consistent with
that field's constraints - and everything already committed elsewhere in the document - can ever be sampled. Nothing
invalid makes it into the output, because it's never given the chance to be sampled in the first place.